PIPE(2)                        P32W Unix                         PIPE(2)

NAME
       pipe - create an interprocess channel

SYNOPSIS
       #include <unistd.h>

       int pipe(int fds[2]);

DESCRIPTION
       pipe makes a pipe, a first-in first-out buffer of 1024
       bytes, and returns two file descriptors for it: fds[0] for
       reading and fds[1] for writing. After a fork(2) the two
       processes can talk through it; the shell connects the
       commands of a pipeline so.

       read(2) on an empty pipe waits for data, and gives end of
       file once every write descriptor is closed. write(2) to a
       full pipe waits for room; to a pipe whose read descriptors
       are all closed it sends SIGPIPE to the writer and fails with
       EPIPE. fstat(2) reports a pipe with mode S_IFIFO | 0600 and
       size 0. A pipe cannot be lseek'd.

RETURN VALUE
       0 on success; -1 on an error, with errno set.

ERRORS
       EMFILE  Fewer than two descriptors are free.
       ENFILE  The system's open-file table is full.
       ENOMEM  There is no memory for the buffer.
       EFAULT  fds is a bad address.

SEE ALSO
       read(2), write(2), fork(2), dup(2), sh(1)

BUGS
       There are no named pipes (FIFOs); see mknod(2).
